        ":question/id": "53548ea4-2b92-5da8-9bc4-e7dc116ebef9",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-96089",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+2y?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(1,1),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=-2+2(1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=1+2(1)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2+2(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/cc/cc55a08f71cd75f0dda356149a61e9ab78536ecbb4644b88f840122863b92b88.png)"
      }
    ],
    ":knowledge-point/title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
  }
